  "how": [
   {
    "h": "Why D-Reducibility?",
    "md": "A configuration is \"arbitrary-reducible\" if there exists *some* valid coloring extension, but the proof may require complex case-by-case reasoning. D-reducibility imposes structure: the configuration can be removed *in a controlled way* using a **discharging argument**, which:\n\n- Assigns initial \"charges\" to vertices based on degree\n- Discharges charges locally according to rules\n- Proves that the final charge distribution contradicts the assumption of a minimal counterexample\n\nThis makes verification algorithmic and parallelizable."
   },
   {
    "h": "The Flat Case Challenge",
    "md": "To find a **linear number** of D-reducible configurations (versus just one for reducibility), the proof must handle the \"flat case\"—regions where many small configurations cluster. This requires:\n- Expanding the configuration set to $>8200$ configurations\n- Using computational verification (since manual checking becomes infeasible)\n- Carefully managing interactions between multiple configurations"
   }
  ],
  "idea": "A **D-reducible configuration** is a local substructure in a planar graph that cannot appear in a minimal counterexample to the Four Color Theorem (4CT). More precisely, a configuration is D-reducible if, whenever it appears in a planar graph $G$, removing the configuration and properly coloring the resulting smaller graph $G'$ guarantees that the coloring can be extended to include the configuration in $G$.\n\nIn the context of 4CT proofs, D-reducibility is a strengthened form of **arbitrarily reducible configurations**, meaning:\n- The configuration admits a *discharging argument* (hence \"D-\") that proves its reducibility\n- It can be verified computationally through finite case analysis\n- Any minimal counterexample to the 4CT cannot contain such a configuration\n\nD-reducible configurations are typically small, localized subgraphs (vertices and their neighborhoods) whose structural properties guarantee Kempe-chain arguments or coloring extensions work correctly.",
  "pitfalls": [
   {
    "h": "Confusion 1: D-reducibility vs. Contractibility",
    "points": [
     "**Mistake**: Assuming D-reducible = simply contractible/removable. **Reality**: A D-reducible configuration may involve *complex Kempe-chain recoloring* in the contracted graph. Removal alone doesn't guarantee the extension works; the discharging argument proves it will."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Confusion 2: \"Linear number\" = One configuration per vertex",
    "points": [
     "**Mistake**: Interpreting $O(n)$ configurations as exactly one per vertex. **Reality**: The count is linear *in the worst case* across the entire graph. Regions may have zero or multiple configurations; linearity is a global property."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Confusion 3: Configuration = Single vertex",
    "points": [
     "**Mistake**: Thinking a configuration is just a high-degree vertex. **Reality**: Configurations are *subgraphs* with structure (vertices, edges, and neighborhood constraints). A configuration might involve 4–6 vertices and specific degree sequences."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Confusion 4: Verification by enumeration is \"trivial\"",
    "points": [
     "Isomorphism checking (configurations may appear in different embeddings)",
     "Kempe-chain propagation (coloring changes cascade globally)",
     "Correctness of the discharging rules"
    ]
   }
  ],
