 "NP-hardness_reduction": {
  "equations": [
   {
    "label": "**Transitivity of NP-hardness:**",
    "note": "where $\\Pi' \\leq_p \\Pi$ denotes the existence of a polynomial-time reduction from $\\Pi'$ to $\\Pi$.",
    "tex": "\\text{If } \\Pi' \\text{ is NP-hard and } \\Pi' \\leq_p \\Pi, \\text{ then } \\Pi \\text{ is NP-hard}"
   },
   {
    "label": "**Reduction runtime constraint:**",
    "note": "This ensures the reduced instance remains \"reasonably sized\" and doesn't hide complexity in the encoding.",
    "tex": "\\text{If } |\\phi(I')| \\leq \\text{poly}(|I'|) \\text{ and } \\phi \\text{ is computable in } O(\\text{poly}(|I'|)), \\text{ then } \\phi \\text{ is valid}"
   }
  ],
  "how": [
   {
    "h": "The Logic",
    "md": "1. **Start with a known hard problem**: Choose $\\Pi'$ from a base set of NP-complete problems (e.g., 3-SAT, Partition, Vertex Cover, Hamiltonian Cycle).\n\n2. **Design the transformation**: Construct $\\phi$ such that:\n   - YES instances map to YES instances\n   - NO instances map to NO instances (or equivalently: satisfiability is preserved)\n\n3. **Verify polynomial time**: Prove $\\phi$ runs in time polynomial in the input size.\n\n4. **Conclude**: $\\Pi$ is NP-hard because if a poly-time algorithm existed for $\\Pi$, it would yield a poly-time algorithm for $\\Pi'$ via $\\phi$, contradicting the assumption that $\\Pi'$ is hard."
   },
   {
    "h": "Subtlety in Optimization Problems",
    "md": "The source material highlights a key challenge: **Partition is a decision problem**, while calibration distance is an **optimization problem**. Reducing to optimization requires extra care:\n\n- For decision problems: preserve YES/NO answers\n- For optimization problems: must show that in NO instances, the optimal value is *strictly worse* by a measurable amount\n\nThis is why the source material states: *\"the crux of the analysis is to argue that, in every 'No' instance of Partition (or variants thereof), the non-existence of 'good' partitions leads to a strictly higher distance from calibration.\"*"
   }
  ],
  "idea": "**NP-hardness reduction** is a technique for proving that a computational problem is NP-hard by demonstrating a polynomial-time transformation (reduction) from a known NP-hard problem to the target problem. \n\nFormally: A problem $\\Pi$ is **NP-hard** if there exists a known NP-hard problem $\\Pi'$ and a polynomial-time computable function $\\phi$ such that:\n- For every instance $I'$ of $\\Pi'$: $I'$ is a YES-instance of $\\Pi'$ $\\Leftrightarrow$ $\\phi(I')$ is a YES-instance of $\\Pi$\n\nThe reduction $\\phi: \\Pi' \\to \\Pi$ is called a **Karp reduction** (or polynomial-time many-one reduction).",
  "pitfalls": [
   {
    "h": "Incorrect Direction of the Reduction",
    "points": [
     "❌ **Mistake**: Reducing from $\\Pi$ (your problem) to $\\Pi'$ (known hard problem). ✓ **Correct**: Reduce FROM the known hard problem TO your problem: $\\Pi' \\leq_p \\Pi$. The direction ensures: if we could solve $\\Pi$ efficiently, we could solve $\\Pi'$ efficiently."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Ignoring Polynomial-Time Constraint",
    "points": [
     "❌ **Mistake**: Transforming instances but allowing exponential-time preprocessing. ✓ **Correct**: Verify that $\\phi$ runs in $O(\\text{poly}(|I'|))$ time, including construction of the reduced instance."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Preserving Only One Direction",
    "points": [
     "❌ **Mistake**: Ensuring YES instances of $\\Pi'$ map to YES instances of $\\Pi$, but forgetting NO instances. ✓ **Correct**: Verify the logical equivalence: $I'$ is YES in $\\Pi'$ $\\Leftrightarrow$ $\\phi(I')$ is YES in $\\Pi$."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Losing Information in Optimization Reductions",
    "points": [
     "❌ **Mistake**: Mapping decision instances to optimization without relating objective values. ✓ **Correct**: Establish that NO instances have strictly worse objective values by a non-negligible gap (as required in the source material)."
    ]
   }
  ],
  "uses": [
   {
    "h": "Computational Complexity Certification",
    "points": [
     "**Machine learning model calibration**: Proving that computing optimal calibrated predictors is NP-hard (as in the source material) establishes fundamental limits on what polynomial-time algorithms can guarantee.",
     "**Resource allocation**: Verifying that bin packing, scheduling, or resource partitioning problems are NP-hard justifies use of heuristics and approximation algorithms in practice."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Algorithm Design Justification",
    "points": [
     "**Constraint satisfaction systems**: When proving a scheduling or configuration problem is NP-hard, engineers know to:",
     "Use dynamic programming with restricted structures (e.g., contiguous intervals)",
     "Deploy approximation algorithms with performance guarantees"
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Hardness-based Security",
    "points": [
     "**Cryptography**: Reductions from NP-hard problems justify the hardness assumptions underlying cryptographic systems.",
     "**Obfuscation**: Complexity-theoretic hardness reductions establish that certain obfuscation tasks are computationally infeasible."
    ]
   },
   {
    "h": "Problem Classification",
    "points": [
     "**Determining problem boundaries**: Reductions help classify whether a new variant of a known problem inherits its hardness (e.g., does allowing contiguous partitions make the problem tractable?)."
    ]
   }
  ],
  "why": {
   "h": "Why Reductions Work",
   "md": "The power of NP-hardness reductions lies in **problem equivalence under transformation**. If problem $\\Pi'$ is \"hard\" (no known efficient algorithm), and we can systematically convert any instance of $\\Pi'$ into an instance of $\\Pi$ while preserving the YES/NO answer, then $\\Pi$ must be at least as hard as $\\Pi'$."
  }
 },
