  "example": {
   "h": "Detailed Walkthrough Example",
   "md": "**Graph:** 6 vertices (0–5), edges: (0,1), (0,3), (1,2), (1,4), (2,5), (3,4), (4,5)\n**Start:** vertex 0\n\n**Step-by-step BFS:**\n\n| Step | Queue | Current | Neighbors | Action | Visited |\n|------|-------|---------|-----------|--------|---------|\n| 1 | [0] | — | — | Enqueue start | {0} |\n| 2 | [1,3] | 0 | 1,3 | Dequeue 0, enqueue neighbors | {0,1,3} |\n| 3 | [3,2,4] | 1 | 2,4 | Dequeue 1, enqueue unvisited neighbors | {0,1,3,2,4} |\n| 4 | [2,4,5] | 3 | 4 (visited) | Dequeue 3, 4 already visited | {0,1,3,2,4} |\n| 5 | [4,5] | 2 | 5 | Dequeue 2, enqueue 5 | {0,1,3,2,4,5} |\n| 6 | [5] | 4 | (all visited) | Dequeue 4, no new neighbors | {0,1,3,2,4,5} |\n| 7 | [] | 5 | (all visited) | Dequeue 5, queue empty → DONE | {0,1,3,2,4,5} |\n\n**BFS Tree produced:**\n```\n       0 (distance 0)\n      / \\\n     1   3 (distance 1)\n    / \\  |\n   2   4 (distance 2)\n    \\ /\n     5 (distance 2)\n```"
  },
  "how": [
   {
    "h": "Pseudocode",
    "md": "```\nprocedure BFS(G, source):\n  queue ← [source]\n  visited ← {source}\n  distance[source] ← 0\n  parent[source] ← null\n  \n  while queue is not empty:\n    u ← queue.dequeue()\n    for each vertex v in adjacency_list[u]:\n      if v not in visited:\n        visited.add(v)\n        distance[v] ← distance[u] + 1\n        parent[v] ← u\n        queue.enqueue(v)\n```"
   }
  ],
  "idea": "**Breadth-first search (BFS)** is a fundamental graph traversal algorithm that explores vertices in a graph by visiting all neighbors of the current vertex before moving to the next level of vertices. Starting from a designated source vertex, BFS systematically explores the graph level by level, maintaining an ordering where vertices closer to the source (in terms of edge count) are visited before more distant vertices.\n\nFormally, BFS constructs a **breadth-first tree** rooted at the source vertex, where each vertex is visited exactly once and stored in a queue data structure for ordered processing.",
